## 3. File-by-file walkthrough

### 3.1 The chaincode (the ledger logic) — `blockchain/chaincode/misinformation/`

This is the *only* part that runs inside the Fabric network.

**`go/misinformation.go`** — the entire chaincode.

- Defines `ReportRecord`, `Vote`, `RegisteredOrg` (the on-chain structs) and
  `MisinformationContract`.
- Uses the modern **`contractapi`** style (not the old `shim` style), so each
  exported method automatically becomes a callable chaincode function.
- `newReportKey` / `newOrgKey` / `newVoteKey` — build composite ledger keys.
- `validateReportInput(...)` — rejects bad rows before anything is written.
- **Consortium review lifecycle (v1.1):**
  - `RegisterOrg` (invoke) — self-enrolls the calling org as a stakeholder.
    Idempotent; returns the MSPID.
  - `SubmitReport` (invoke) — creates a **PENDING** report. Only a registered
    org may submit; records `submitted_by` from the MSP.
  - `CastVote` (invoke) — one org's `accept`/`reject` verdict on a PENDING
    report. One vote per org per report (enforced by the `vote:` composite key).
  - `FinalizeReport` (invoke) — locks the report once **≥ 2/3 of registered
    orgs** have voted: `FINAL` if quorum accepted, `REJECTED` otherwise. After
    this the record is immutable and votes are closed.
- **Queries (all read-only):**
  - `QueryReport` / `QueryAllReports` / `GetReportCount` — fetch records.
  - `QueryVotes` — the vote tally for a report.
  - `QueryReportHistory` — ledger key history = the tamper-evident audit trail.
  - `ComputeContentHash` — helper that hashes text the same way the Python
    bridge does, so hashes can be verified.

**`go/go.mod` / `go/go.sum`** — Go module definition; pins
`fabric-chaincode-go/v2` and `fabric-contract-api-go/v2`.

**`go/misinformation_test.go`** — unit tests (submit/query round-trip, duplicate
rejection = immutability, input-validation rejections, count/all queries, hash
helper). Run with `cd go && go test ./...`.

**`go/mockstub_test.go`** — a vendored Apache-2.0 in-memory `MockStub` (from
fabric-contract-api-go's own functional tests) so the chaincode can be tested
without a running network. Implements state, range/partial-key queries, history,
and a configurable fake X.509 identity (per-MSP) so multi-org voting can be
tested without a network.

**`go/vendor/`** — auto-generated by Fabric's `deployCC` during packaging
(`go mod vendor`). Safe to delete; regenerated on every deploy and gitignored.

**`DATA_MODEL.md`** — the written-down data-model decision record (B1).
**`README.md`** — quick overview of design, functions, topology, scripts.
**`DEPLOYMENT.md`** — Colab/Kaggle packaging notes (C2).

### 3.2 The lifecycle scripts — `blockchain/scripts/`

**`deploy.sh`** — one-command network bring-up + chaincode deployment (B4–B6).

- Checks `jq` is available (the Fabric scripts need it).
- `./scripts/deploy.sh` → `network.sh up` (starts 2-org Raft network) →
  `createChannel` → `deployCC` (package → install on **both** orgs → approve on
  **both** orgs → commit). Endorsement policy: `AND(Org1, Org2)`.
- `./scripts/deploy.sh down` → tears the network down.

**`onboard-org3.sh`** — robustness test: adds a 3rd stakeholder org.

- Packages the chaincode, installs on all 3 peers, approves on all 3, and
  re-commits with **`OutOf(2, Org1MSP, Org2MSP, Org3MSP)`** — a 2-of-3
  endorsement quorum. Requires `test-network/addOrg3/addOrg3.sh up` first.
- After this, the bridge/smoke-test use `org3` as an endorser and voter.

**`smoke-test.sh`** — CLI smoke test proving the deployed chaincode works (B5).

- Switches signing identity between org1/org2/org3 (`use_org`), so the test
  exercises real multi-org behaviour.
- Runs the full consortium review flow: `RegisterOrg` (all orgs) →
  `SubmitReport` → `CastVote` (all orgs) → `FinalizeReport` → queries
  (`QueryReport`, `QueryVotes`, `QueryAllReports`, `GetReportCount`,
  `QueryReportHistory`).
- Every invoke targets a 2-of-3 endorsing peer set (org1+org2, which satisfies
  the `OutOf(2,...)` policy) with `--waitForEvent`.
- This is the CLI-only replacement for the REST API layer (B5: REST deferred).

### 3.3 The bridge — `apps/ai_service/v1-0-0/app/src/blockchain.py`

C1 — the Python → chaincode connection. Two pieces:

- **`Prediction`** (dataclass) — validates a prediction and hashes the raw text
  with SHA-256 (`Prediction.hash_content`), matching `ComputeContentHash`
  on-chain. Raw text is never sent over the wire.
- **`FabricBridge`** — a thin wrapper around the `peer` CLI. Builds the correct
  `CORE_PEER_*` env vars from the test-network's crypto material, then shells out
  to `peer chaincode invoke/query`.
  - `org=` selects the signing identity (`org1`/`org2`/`org3`);
    `endorsers=` picks which peers endorse (default `["org1","org2"]` = 2 of
    the 2/3 quorum after org3 onboarding).
  - `invoke()` adds `--waitForEvent` so commits complete before returning.
  - Review-workflow methods: `register_org`, `list_orgs`, `submit_report`,
    `cast_vote(language, row_id, verdict)`, `finalize_report`.
  - Query methods: `query_report`, `query_all`, `count`, `votes`, `history`.
- **`submit_pipeline_output(rows, language, model_version, bridge)`** — batch
  helper that takes pipeline rows `[{row_id, text, label, confidence}]`, hashes
  each, and submits them as PENDING reports.

Verified against the live network: register → submit → vote → finalize, plus
count/query/history/hash equality (on-chain sha256 == Python sha256) all
confirmed.

